-- Q1514: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only for publicly traded companies subject to PCAOB oversight","Only when the auditor suspects fraud has occurred in the period","Only when the auditor intends to rely on controls as part of the audit","For every audit engagement, regardless of the planned control reliance strategy"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): AU-C 315 requires the auditor to obtain an understanding of internal control relevant to the audit for every engagement, regardless of whether the auditor plans to rely on controls. This understanding helps identify potential misstatements and design the nature, timing, and extent of further audit procedures.
Wrong (A): The requirement applies to all audits, not just publicly traded companies. Both PCAOB and AICPA standards mandate this understanding.
Wrong (B): Understanding internal control is required for every engagement, not only when fraud is suspected.
Wrong (C): Even when the auditor takes a purely substantive approach, an understanding of internal control is still required under AU-C 315.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1514;

-- Q1516: Risk Assessment [medium/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reviewing accounting estimates for biases indicating possible fraud","Observing the client''s physical inventory count procedures","Confirming outstanding accounts payable balances with vendors","Performing a comprehensive search for unrecorded liabilities"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): AU-C 240 requires three specific procedures to address management override risk: testing journal entries and other adjustments, reviewing accounting estimates for biases, and evaluating the business rationale for significant unusual transactions. Reviewing estimates for bias helps detect manipulation of assumptions.
Wrong (B): Observing inventory counts addresses existence and valuation assertions but is not one of the three required management override procedures under AU-C 240.
Wrong (C): Confirming payables addresses completeness and existence but does not specifically target management override risk.
Wrong (D): Searching for unrecorded liabilities addresses completeness of liabilities but is not among the three required override procedures.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1516;

-- Q1517: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether the entity''s competitors are publicly traded or privately held","Only current year transactions without considering broader industry trends","Only the entity''s historical financial performance and past audit results","The potential impact on asset values, going concern, and inventory obsolescence"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315.A37-A39, inherent risk factors include the nature of the entity''s industry and economic conditions. Rapid technological change and declining demand increase the risk that inventory may be obsolete, long-lived assets may be impaired, and going concern may be in doubt.
Wrong (A): The competitors'' public or private status is not a relevant inherent risk factor for assessing the impact of technological change on the entity.
Wrong (B): Limiting consideration to current year transactions ignores significant industry-level conditions that affect multiple assertions and periods.
Wrong (C): Historical performance alone is insufficient; the auditor must also consider current and prospective conditions that affect future asset recoverability.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1517;

-- Q1518: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["IT general controls, application controls, and system interface error risks","Only general controls over the primary financial reporting application system","Whether the entity currently uses cloud-based systems for data storage","Only the specific controls tested by the entity''s own IT audit team"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 315.A54-A56, when assessing risks in a complex IT environment with multiple integrated systems, the auditor should consider IT general controls, application controls, and the risks that interfaces between systems may introduce errors through data translation or timing issues.
Wrong (B): Focusing solely on the primary system ignores interface risks and application controls in the other integrated systems that process financial data.
Wrong (C): Whether the entity uses cloud-based systems is only one aspect of IT risk; the auditor must evaluate the full range of ITGCs and application controls.
Wrong (D): The auditor cannot limit the assessment to controls the entity''s IT team has tested; independent evaluation of all relevant IT controls is required.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1518;

-- Q1519: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Communicate the identified risk directly to the entity''s legal counsel","Issue a modified audit report disclosing the significant inventory risk","Design further audit procedures responsive to the assessed risk level","Always perform both tests of controls and substantive procedures together"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330, when risks are identified at the assertion level, the auditor must design and perform further audit procedures whose nature, timing, and extent are responsive to those specific risks. The auditor has flexibility to choose a combined or substantive-only approach.
Wrong (A): Communicating risks to legal counsel is not the auditor''s required response to assessed risks; the auditor must design responsive audit procedures.
Wrong (B): A modified report addresses the opinion on financial statements, not the auditor''s procedural response to identified risks during fieldwork.
Wrong (D): The auditor is not required to perform both tests of controls and substantive procedures; a substantive-only approach may be appropriate for some risks.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1519;

-- Q1520: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Valuation of revenue and cost estimates on long-term construction contracts","Presentation and disclosure of the entity''s fixed asset classifications","Completeness of cash disbursements recorded during the construction period","Existence of office equipment and other administrative fixed assets"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): The percentage-of-completion method requires significant estimates of total contract revenue and costs, which directly affect revenue recognition timing and amounts. These estimates involve substantial judgment and are inherently susceptible to misstatement, making the valuation assertion high risk.
Wrong (B): Fixed asset presentation and disclosure involves relatively straightforward classification with lower estimation uncertainty than long-term contract estimates.
Wrong (C): Cash disbursement completeness is a routine assertion that does not involve the same level of judgment as percentage-of-completion estimates.
Wrong (D): Office equipment existence can typically be verified through physical observation and carries lower inherent risk than complex revenue estimates.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1520;

-- Q1523: Risk Assessment [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Perform no substantive procedures since assessed control risk is low","Test controls to confirm low control risk, then perform targeted substantive work","Perform only analytical procedures without any detailed substantive testing","Set detection risk at high since the overall combined audit risk is moderate"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 330, when inherent risk is high but control risk is assessed as low, the auditor should test controls to confirm they are operating effectively, then design reduced but targeted substantive procedures. The auditor cannot eliminate substantive procedures entirely, even when controls are effective.
Wrong (A): AU-C 330.06 requires substantive procedures for all relevant assertions regardless of how low control risk is assessed, so eliminating them entirely is never appropriate.
Wrong (C): Analytical procedures alone may be insufficient when inherent risk is high; the auditor should perform targeted substantive detail testing for high-risk assertions.
Wrong (D): Setting detection risk at high when inherent risk is high would result in an unacceptably high audit risk, violating the audit risk model requirements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1523;

-- Q1525: Risk Assessment [hard/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Address all significant risks with equal procedures regardless of assessed likelihood","Develop a tailored response for each risk based on its nature and assertions affected","Focus audit resources exclusively on the single largest identified significant risk","Defer all significant risks to the subsequent year''s audit for further evaluation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 330, each significant risk requires a tailored audit response based on its specific characteristics. The auditor considers the nature of the risk, which assertions are affected, the likely magnitude of potential misstatement, and whether the risk relates to fraud.
Wrong (A): A one-size-fits-all approach fails to address the unique characteristics of each significant risk and would not comply with AU-C 330''s requirement for responsive procedures.
Wrong (C): Ignoring all but one significant risk would leave other areas of material misstatement unaddressed, violating the auditor''s responsibility under AU-C 315.
Wrong (D): Significant risks require a current-period response; deferring them entirely would leave known risks unaddressed and violate professional standards.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1525;

-- Q1526: Risk Assessment [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only opportunity due to the CEO''s dual leadership role in the organization","Detection risk and control risk but not any fraud triangle condition directly","Only rationalization because management can justify any financial reporting outcome","Incentive from compensation structure and opportunity from weak governance oversight"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 240, the CEO''s compensation tied to earnings targets creates incentive/pressure to manipulate earnings, while the dual role as CEO and chairman represents weak governance oversight (opportunity) because the board''s independent monitoring of management is compromised. This combination significantly increases fraud risk.
Wrong (A): Opportunity is present, but the compensation structure also creates a clear incentive/pressure condition that must not be overlooked in the fraud risk assessment.
Wrong (B): Detection risk and control risk are audit risk model components, not fraud triangle conditions; the scenario describes incentive and opportunity specifically.
Wrong (C): While rationalization may exist, the scenario most directly illustrates incentive (earnings-based compensation) and opportunity (weak governance), not rationalization alone.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1526;

-- Q1527: Risk Assessment [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Straight-line depreciation of office furniture with a known useful life estimate","Fair value of Level 3 instruments requiring significant unobservable inputs","Accrued utility expenses estimated from the prior month''s billing amounts","Prepaid insurance amounts calculated directly from the insurance policy terms"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 540, Level 3 financial instruments have the highest estimation uncertainty because their fair value relies on significant unobservable inputs and complex valuation models. Unlike Level 1 or Level 2 instruments, Level 3 valuations involve substantial judgment, making them highly susceptible to misstatement.
Wrong (A): Straight-line depreciation with a known useful life involves minimal estimation uncertainty since the calculation inputs are well established and objective.
Wrong (C): Accrued utility expenses based on prior billing involve low uncertainty because the amounts are based on observable, recent historical data.
Wrong (D): Prepaid insurance calculated from policy terms involves minimal judgment since the amounts are contractually specified and readily determinable.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1527;

-- Q1529: Risk Assessment [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["IT risks as low because legacy systems are inherently more reliable than newer ones","A high risk that IT general controls are ineffective, requiring a substantive approach","IT risks as irrelevant to the financial statement audit of this particular entity","Only the risks related to the newest IT systems deployed in the current period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 315.A54-A56, outdated security patches, shared administrator passwords, and no change management process indicate significant ITGC weaknesses. These deficiencies could allow unauthorized changes to data and programs, undermining the reliability of automated controls and potentially requiring a substantive-only audit approach.
Wrong (A): Legacy systems with outdated patches and poor access controls are not more reliable; they present significant security and data integrity risks.
Wrong (C): IT risks directly affect the reliability of financial data processing and are always relevant to a financial statement audit under AU-C 315.
Wrong (D): The auditor must assess IT risks across all systems that process financial data, not just the newest ones, especially when legacy systems have known control weaknesses.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1529;

-- Q1530: Risk Assessment [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the policy differences as management''s prerogative without further inquiry","Evaluate whether the differences indicate aggressive accounting or management bias","Change the entity''s accounting policies to conform with industry standard norms","Issue a qualified opinion based solely on the existence of the policy differences"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 315, significant departures from industry-standard accounting policies may indicate aggressive accounting, management bias, or attempts to manipulate financial results. The auditor should evaluate whether the policies are within the acceptable range under the framework and whether they increase misstatement risk.
Wrong (A): Accepting differences without further inquiry fails to meet the auditor''s obligation to evaluate whether unusual policies indicate bias or heightened risk.
Wrong (C): The auditor has no authority to change the entity''s accounting policies; the auditor''s role is to evaluate whether they comply with the applicable framework.
Wrong (D): Policy differences alone do not automatically warrant a qualified opinion; the auditor must first evaluate whether the policies are acceptable under the framework.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1530;

-- Q1531: Risk Assessment [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Rely on prior year test results since the underlying control objectives remain unchanged","Reassess control design in the new ERP and test ITGCs before relying on automation","Skip testing automated controls entirely and perform only substantive audit procedures","Accept the ERP vendor''s assurance that the system''s built-in controls are effective"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 315, a new ERP system fundamentally changes the control environment even if control objectives remain the same. The auditor must reassess control design and implementation, perform new walkthroughs, and evaluate IT general controls over the new system before placing reliance on automated controls.
Wrong (A): Prior year test results are not relevant when the underlying system has changed; new controls in a new system require fresh evaluation regardless of objectives.
Wrong (C): While a substantive-only approach is possible, the auditor should first assess whether the new system''s controls can be relied upon, which may allow a more efficient audit.
Wrong (D): Vendor assurances do not constitute sufficient audit evidence; the auditor must independently evaluate and test the controls in the new ERP system.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1531;

-- Q1535: Internal Controls [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["One employee records all purchase transactions and also approves the payments","One employee handles every aspect of the payroll cycle from start to finish","The employee receiving cash also prepares and reviews the bank reconciliation","Different employees handle authorization, recording, and custody of assets"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315.A78, proper segregation of duties separates the functions of authorization, recording, and custody. When different employees handle each function, the risk of errors or fraud is reduced because no single individual controls all aspects of a transaction.
Wrong (A): Having the same employee record and approve transactions combines recording and authorization functions, violating segregation of duties principles.
Wrong (B): One employee handling all payroll aspects combines authorization, recording, and custody, creating significant fraud and error risk.
Wrong (C): The employee receiving cash (custody) should not also prepare the bank reconciliation (recording/verification), as this combination enables concealment of theft.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1535;

-- Q1537: Internal Controls [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Approving transactions before they are processed by the accounting department","Physically safeguarding assets from theft, loss, or unauthorized access","Setting the ethical tone at the top of the organization for all employees","Ongoing and separate evaluations of whether internal control components function"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315.A91, the monitoring component of the COSO framework involves ongoing evaluations, separate evaluations, or a combination of both to ascertain whether the five components of internal control are present and functioning. Ongoing monitoring is built into normal operations.
Wrong (A): Approving transactions before processing describes a control activity (authorization), not the monitoring component of internal control.
Wrong (B): Physically safeguarding assets describes a control activity related to the safeguarding of assets, not the monitoring component.
Wrong (C): Setting the ethical tone at the top is part of the control environment component, not the monitoring component of the COSO framework.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1537;

-- Q1540: Internal Controls [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Communication flows only downward from top management to operating staff","Only financial information is communicated to employees and external parties","Relevant information is captured and communicated enabling people to act on it","All internal communications must be formally documented in writing and retained"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 315.A94, the information and communication component requires the entity to identify, capture, and communicate relevant information in a form and timeframe that enables people to carry out their responsibilities. Effective communication flows in all directions throughout the organization.
Wrong (A): Restricting communication to only downward flows would undermine effectiveness; AU-C 315.A95 requires communication to flow upward, downward, and across the organization.
Wrong (B): The information and communication component encompasses all relevant information, including both financial and nonfinancial data needed for effective operations.
Wrong (D): While documentation supports the component, requiring all communications to be in writing is not a COSO requirement and would be impractical for daily operations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1540;

-- Q1544: Internal Controls [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Identify and analyze risks to objectives as a basis for managing those risks","Perform external audits annually to validate the financial reporting process","Assign an individual risk rating to every employee within the organization","Eliminate all risks that could potentially affect the entity''s financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 315.A85, the risk assessment component of COSO internal control requires the entity to identify and analyze risks relevant to the achievement of its objectives. This involves assessing significance, likelihood, and determining appropriate risk responses.
Wrong (B): External audits are not a component of the entity''s internal risk assessment process; the risk assessment component is an internal management responsibility.
Wrong (C): Risk assessment focuses on risks to organizational objectives, not individual employee risk ratings, which is not a COSO framework requirement.
Wrong (D): The goal of risk assessment is risk management, not risk elimination; some level of risk is inherent in all business activities and cannot be fully eliminated.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1544;

-- Q1546: Internal Controls [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only items selected from the last month of the audit period under review","Exactly 25 items regardless of population size or control operation frequency","Large enough to provide sufficient evidence of effectiveness throughout the period","A single observation of the control being performed on one selected transaction"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330.A25, when testing a manual control performed daily, the auditor needs a sample large enough to provide sufficient evidence that the control operated effectively throughout the entire audit period. AU-C 530.A6 requires considering both frequency and period length.
Wrong (A): Testing only the last month ignores the remaining months and cannot provide evidence that the control operated consistently throughout the full period.
Wrong (B): A fixed sample size of 25 does not consider the frequency of the control or the length of the period, which are required considerations under AU-C 530.
Wrong (D): A single observation cannot provide sufficient evidence of consistent operation over an entire period for a daily manual control.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1546;

-- Q1548: Internal Controls [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A factor that only affects the monitoring component of internal control","A strength because less board involvement allows greater management autonomy","A control environment weakness that may increase financial statement level risk","Irrelevant because the board of directors is not involved in daily operations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 315.A77, the control environment sets the tone of the organization and is the foundation for all other components. AU-C 315.A78 identifies governance oversight as a key element, and limited board involvement weakens oversight, increasing risk that management may override controls.
Wrong (A): Board oversight is part of the control environment, not just the monitoring component; its weakness affects the foundation of the entire control system.
Wrong (B): Reduced board involvement is a weakness, not a strength; management autonomy without oversight increases the risk of fraud and financial reporting manipulation.
Wrong (D): The board''s oversight role is directly relevant to financial reporting quality even though the board does not participate in daily operations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1548;

-- Q1549: Internal Controls [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The control has been operating effectively and consistently for the entire period","The control is capable of effectively preventing or detecting material misstatements","The control has received formal approval from the entity''s board of directors","The control produces sufficient documentary evidence for the auditor to examine"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 315.14, testing design effectiveness evaluates whether a control, individually or in combination with other controls, is capable of effectively preventing or detecting and correcting material misstatements. This assessment occurs before testing operating effectiveness.
Wrong (A): Whether the control has been operating consistently is a test of operating effectiveness, which is a separate evaluation from design effectiveness.
Wrong (C): Board approval is not relevant to design effectiveness; the evaluation focuses on whether the control can achieve its intended objective.
Wrong (D): While documentary evidence may support testing, design effectiveness is about the control''s capability to address misstatements, not its documentation output.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1549;

-- Q1550: Internal Controls [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Conclude the control is effective since the observed error rate is only 5%","Evaluate the nature and cause of exceptions and their impact on control risk","Immediately communicate a material weakness to those charged with governance","Accept the 5% error rate as immaterial to the overall audit conclusions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 330.A24, when exceptions are found in tests of controls, the auditor must evaluate the nature and cause of the exceptions. AU-C 530.13 requires considering whether deviations indicate a systematic breakdown and whether control risk should be revised.
Wrong (A): Simply computing an error rate without analyzing the nature and cause of exceptions does not satisfy the auditor''s evaluation requirements under AU-C 330.
Wrong (C): Immediately communicating a material weakness without first evaluating the nature, cause, and significance of the exceptions would be premature.
Wrong (D): Ignoring control exceptions entirely would undermine the purpose of testing controls and could result in an inappropriately low assessment of control risk.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1550;

-- Q1552: Internal Controls [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The entity''s human resources policies and employee hiring procedures","The auditor''s independence and professional objectivity requirements","Physical controls over tangible assets and inventory in the warehouse","The reliability of automated controls and integrity of processed data"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315.A107, IT general controls including change management over application software support the continued proper functioning of information processing controls. Without formal change management, unauthorized modifications could introduce processing errors and compromise data integrity.
Wrong (A): Human resources policies relate to competence and personnel management, which are separate from IT change management controls over software modifications.
Wrong (B): The auditor''s independence is governed by ethics standards and is not affected by the entity''s IT change management deficiencies.
Wrong (C): Physical controls over tangible assets relate to safeguarding, not to the integrity of application software processing and automated controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1552;

-- Q1556: Internal Controls [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A deficiency that has already resulted in an actual material financial misstatement","Any control deficiency identified by the auditor during the course of the audit","A deficiency creating a reasonable possibility that material misstatement goes undetected","A deficiency that the entity''s management has formally acknowledged in a written response"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 265, a material weakness is a deficiency, or combination of deficiencies, in internal control such that there is a reasonable possibility that a material misstatement will not be prevented, or detected and corrected, on a timely basis. This is the most severe classification.
Wrong (A): A material weakness does not require an actual misstatement to have occurred; the threshold is a reasonable possibility that one could go undetected.
Wrong (B): Not every control deficiency qualifies as a material weakness; only those meeting the reasonable-possibility-of-material-misstatement threshold are classified as such.
Wrong (D): Management''s acknowledgment does not define a material weakness; the classification is based on severity relative to the reasonable possibility threshold.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1556;

-- Q1559: Internal Controls [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Management verbally assures the auditor that monitoring regularly occurs","The entity has recently hired additional accounting and finance staff members","Internal audit regularly evaluates controls, reports findings, and tracks remediation","The entity maintains a written policy that requires monitoring of all controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 315.A86-A87, monitoring activities assess whether internal controls are present and functioning over time. An active internal audit function that regularly evaluates controls, reports to the audit committee, and tracks remediation of deficiencies provides the strongest evidence of effective monitoring.
Wrong (A): Management''s verbal assurance is insufficient without corroborating evidence of systematic evaluation; oral representations alone do not demonstrate effective monitoring.
Wrong (B): Hiring additional staff addresses capacity but does not constitute systematic monitoring of internal control effectiveness over time.
Wrong (D): A written policy establishes intent but does not by itself demonstrate that monitoring is actually being performed and that deficiencies are being addressed.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1559;

-- Q1561: Internal Controls [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Physical control that restricts employee access to sensitive asset areas","Detective control within the control environment component of COSO","Preventive control designed to stop information processing errors before they occur","Detective control activity that uncovers irregularities during the employee''s absence"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315.A73, mandatory vacation policies are detective controls. When an employee is absent, another person must perform their duties, which may reveal irregularities such as fraud or errors that the absent employee had been concealing.
Wrong (A): Physical controls restrict access to assets; mandatory vacation is not about physical access but about requiring job rotation to detect concealed irregularities.
Wrong (B): The control environment refers to governance and tone at the top, not specific detective controls like mandatory vacation policies.
Wrong (C): Preventive controls stop errors before they occur; mandatory vacation is a detective control that uncovers problems after the fact through substitute performance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1561;

-- Q1562: Internal Controls [medium/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Obtain and evaluate a SOC 1 Type 2 report covering the service organization''s controls","Ignore the service organization''s controls as they fall outside the entity''s control","Require the user entity to change to a different payroll service organization","Visit the service organization directly and perform all control testing independently"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 402, a SOC 1 Type 2 report provides information about the design and operating effectiveness of controls at the service organization. The auditor evaluates the report''s scope, the service auditor''s opinion, test results, and complementary user entity controls.
Wrong (B): AU-C 402 requires the auditor to consider the service organization''s controls when they are relevant to the audit; ignoring them entirely is not appropriate.
Wrong (C): The auditor has no authority to require the entity to change service providers; the role is to evaluate the existing control environment.
Wrong (D): While visiting the service organization is an option, obtaining a SOC 1 Type 2 report is typically the most practical and efficient approach under AU-C 402.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1562;

-- Q1564: Internal Controls [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Confirm understanding of transaction flow and evaluate whether controls are implemented","Test the operating effectiveness of the revenue cycle controls over the full period","Count the total number of sales transactions processed during the audit period","Determine the appropriate sample size needed for substantive revenue testing"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 315, a walkthrough involves tracing one or a few transactions through the entire process from initiation to recording. Its primary purpose is to confirm the auditor''s understanding of the transaction flow, identify control points, and evaluate whether controls have been implemented.
Wrong (B): Testing operating effectiveness requires sampling over a period and is a separate procedure from a walkthrough, which evaluates design and implementation.
Wrong (C): Counting transactions is not the purpose of a walkthrough; walkthroughs trace a single transaction to understand the process flow and controls.
Wrong (D): Determining sample sizes for substantive testing is a planning decision based on risk assessment, not the objective of performing a walkthrough.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1564;

-- Q1567: Internal Controls [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether the deficiency was caused by management decisions or by employee errors","Severity: material weakness has reasonable possibility of material misstatement","Whether the deficiency has already been reported to federal or state regulators","The estimated remediation cost of the deficiency relative to total audit fees"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 265, the distinction between a significant deficiency and a material weakness is based on severity. A material weakness exists when there is a reasonable possibility that a material misstatement will not be prevented or detected on a timely basis. A significant deficiency is less severe but still merits governance attention.
Wrong (A): The cause of the deficiency (management vs. employee) does not determine the classification; severity of potential impact on financial statements is the key factor.
Wrong (C): Whether the deficiency has been reported to regulators is irrelevant to the classification; the distinction is based on the likelihood and magnitude of potential misstatement.
Wrong (D): Remediation cost relative to audit fees is not a criterion for distinguishing between significant deficiencies and material weaknesses under AU-C 265.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1567;

-- Q1568: Internal Controls [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The entity has implemented the maximum number of controls that are possible","Entity-level controls operate with precision sufficient to address misstatement risk","Each entity-level control has been independently tested by an outside third party","Entity-level controls produce paper documentation that can be physically inspected"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 315, entity-level controls vary in their precision. The auditor evaluates whether they operate at a level of precision sufficient to prevent or detect material misstatements. Some entity-level controls (like detailed management reviews) may be precise enough to address assertion-level risks.
Wrong (A): The quantity of controls is not the relevant consideration; the auditor evaluates whether controls operate with sufficient precision to address misstatement risk.
Wrong (C): Third-party testing is not required for entity-level controls; the auditor independently evaluates the controls'' design and precision during the audit.
Wrong (D): Paper documentation is not the defining characteristic of effective entity-level controls; many effective controls operate through electronic or verbal processes.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1568;

-- Q1570: Internal Controls [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Control deficiencies may go undetected, allowing other components to deteriorate","The entity''s financial statements will automatically contain material misstatements","The auditor is required to resign from the engagement due to scope limitations","Weak monitoring has no effect since the other four COSO components remain strong"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 315.A86-A87, weak monitoring creates a risk that deficiencies in other control components will not be identified and corrected on a timely basis. Even strong controls can deteriorate over time due to personnel, process, or technology changes, and without effective monitoring these deteriorations go undetected.
Wrong (B): Weak monitoring does not automatically result in material misstatements; it increases the risk that control failures will not be caught, which may eventually lead to misstatements.
Wrong (C): A weak monitoring component does not constitute a scope limitation requiring resignation; the auditor adjusts the audit approach to address the increased risk.
Wrong (D): The five COSO components work together as an integrated system; weakness in monitoring undermines the reliability of the other components over time.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1570;
